# Recent activity data sources

## Why

Two indexer endpoints provide pre-sorted, paginated feeds:

- `GET /api/v1/transactions/latest` → `useLatestTransactions`
- `GET /api/v1/transfers/latest` → `useLatestTransfers`

Both use cursor-based pagination via TanStack Query's `useInfiniteQuery`. Transfers can be filtered by `eventType[]` (`VET`, `FUNGIBLE_TOKEN`, `NFT`, `SEMI_FUNGIBLE_TOKEN`).

There is no indexer endpoint for "latest blocks", so the blocks listing still scans Thor — but only the `/activity/blocks` page (which needs per-tx data for clauses/VTHO paid) uses expanded blocks. The homepage Blocks card uses the lighter compressed form.

## Architecture

```
useLatestTransactions({ size, expanded })   ← /api/v1/transactions/latest
  ├─ Homepage Transactions card             ← size=5, expanded=false
  └─ /activity/transactions page            ← cursor pagination

useLatestTransfers({ size, eventType })     ← /api/v1/transfers/latest
  ├─ Homepage Token Transfers card          ← size=5, no filter (all event types)
  └─ /transfers page                        ← cursor pagination + filter chips

useRecentBlocksCompressed({ count })        ← Thor compressed blocks
  └─ Homepage Blocks card                   ← count=5, no per-tx data needed

useRecentBlocksExpanded({ count })          ← Thor expanded blocks
  └─ /activity/blocks page                  ← needs tx clauses + paid for showDetails
```

## Cursor pagination pattern

`useInfiniteQuery` returns `data.pages: PageResponse[]` where each page has `data` and `pagination.cursor`. To wrap this in classic numeric pagination:

```tsx
const { data, hasNextPage, fetchNextPage, isFetchingNextPage } = useLatestTransfers({ size: pageSize })
const pages = data?.pages ?? []
const currentPageData = pages[page]?.data ?? []
const hasNext = page < pages.length - 1 || Boolean(hasNextPage)

const handlePageChange = async (newPage: number) => {
  if (newPage > pages.length - 1 && hasNextPage && !isFetchingNextPage) {
    await fetchNextPage()
  }
  setPage(newPage)
}
```

Going forward fetches a new cursor; going back is free (already cached). Changing `pageSize` or `eventType` resets the query (different `queryKey`).

## Block fetching (Thor)

`useRecentBlocksCompressed` and `useRecentBlocksExpanded` both fan out to N individual `getBlock(revision)` calls cached individually by `[network, blockNumber]` with `staleTime: Infinity`. Expanded variant has a +10 progressive step in case some blocks failed to load.

Use compressed unless you need per-transaction data (clauses, paid, gasUsed-per-tx).
